   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Vowel length",
     "texto": [
      "In Dutch, the length of a vowel can completely change the meaning of a word. A double vowel (aa, oo, ee, uu), or a single vowel in an open syllable, makes a long sound, as in «maan» (moon) or «boom» (tree). By contrast, a single vowel in a closed syllable is short, as in «man» (man) or «bom» (bomb). English speakers usually tell words apart by vowel quality (as in «ship» and «sheep»), so this is a new habit for them. Hearing and saying these minimal pairs is essential to avoid serious confusion in everyday communication."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
  },
